---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { InfoAlert } from "../../components/react/ui";
---

<Layout
  role="visitor"
  breadcrumbs={[
    { label: "Home", href: "/" },
    { label: "Methods & quality" },
    { label: "Data quality & curation" },
  ]}
  backHref="/methods/evaluation-framework"
>
  <div class="mx-auto px-4 py-8 max-w-4xl prose prose-gray">
    <h1>Data Quality &amp; Curation</h1>

    <InfoAlert title="Placeholder content" variant="warning" client:load>
      <p>The curator role and review cadence must be confirmed by the consortium.
      Current values are placeholders.</p>
    </InfoAlert>

    <section class="mt-6">
      <h2>Data validation workflow</h2>
      <p>
        All data submitted to the SUM platform passes through a structured validation process
        before being made available for analysis and display. This ensures data integrity and
        comparability across cities.
      </p>
      <p>The validation workflow consists of:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Format validation</strong> — Submitted values are checked against expected data
          types, units, and ranges for each KPI.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Completeness check</strong> — Required fields and minimum data requirements
          are verified before a submission is accepted.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Plausibility review</strong> — Values are compared against known ranges and
          against other submissions from the same city to detect anomalies.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Consortium review</strong> — The data curation team performs a final check
          and confirms the submission as validated.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Curator role</h2>
      <p>
        Data quality is overseen by the <strong>SUM Data Curation Team</strong> (placeholder —
        consortium to confirm the named role and responsible person or institution).
      </p>
      <p>
        The curator team is responsible for:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Reviewing submitted data for plausibility and completeness</li>
        <li>Coordinating with cities to resolve data quality issues</li>
        <li>Approving data for publication on the platform</li>
        <li>Maintaining the validation standards documentation</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Review cadence</h2>
      <p>
        Data is reviewed on a <strong>Quarterly review cycle</strong> (placeholder — consortium
        to confirm). Cities are encouraged to submit data ahead of each review window to ensure
        timely inclusion in platform analyses.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>How quality is communicated to users</h2>
      <p>
        The platform uses several visual indicators to communicate data quality:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Evidence strength badges</strong> — Show the normalised ratio of data coverage
          for statistical association results. Three levels: Limited, Moderate, Strong.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Freshness indicators</strong> — Show whether a city's data was recently validated
          or whether it may be outdated.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Curated domain badge</strong> — KPI groups flagged as curated are pre-selected
          as the default view because of their particular relevance to NSM integration.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Validation date</strong> — The date a city's data was last reviewed and confirmed
          by the consortium team.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <nav class="not-prose mt-8 flex gap-4 text-sm">
      <a href="/methods/models" class="text-primary hover:underline">Next: How the models work →</a>
    </nav>
  </div>
</Layout>
